<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-orthogonal-complements-and-projections">
  <title>Orthogonal projection</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We introduce orthogonal projection and then use it to derive the Gram–Schmidt algorithm.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-projections">
    <title>Definition and formula</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-orthogonal-complement">
      <title>The orthogonal complement of a subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>U</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, the <term>orthogonal complement</term> of <m>U</m> is the subset of <m>\R^n</m> given by
          <me>U^\perp = \{\mathbf{x} \text{ in } \R^n \mid \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{y} = 0 \text{ for all } \mathbf{y} \text{ in } U\}</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-6-2-orthogonal-complement-properties">
      <title>Properties of orthogonal complements</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following properties of the <xref ref="def-orthogonal-complement" text="custom">orthogonal complement</xref> hold:
          <ol>
            <li>For any subspace <m>U</m> of <m>\R^n</m>, <m>U^\perp</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m></li>
            <li><m>\{\mathbf{0}\}^\perp = \R^n</m> and <m>(\R^n)^\perp = \{\mathbf{0}\}</m></li>
            <li>If <m>U = \spans \{\mathbf{x}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_k\}</m>, then 
            <me>U^\perp = \{\mathbf{x} \text{ in } \R^n \mid \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{x}_i = 0 \text{ for } i=1,2,\ldots,k\}</me></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <activity>
      <title>Finding an orthogonal complement (U4-LO2)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>U^\perp</m> if <m>U = \spans \{(1,1,0), (0,1,1)\}</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The third property of <xref ref="thm-6-2-orthogonal-complement-properties"/> implies that a vector <m>\mathbf{w} = (x,y,z)</m> lies in <m>U^\perp</m> if and only if it is orthogonal to both <m>(1,1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1,1)</m>. Taking these two dot products gives
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{w}\cdot(1,1,0)=x+y \amp = 0</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{w}\cdot(0,1,1)=y+z \amp = 0.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          With columns in the order <m>x,y,z</m>, the augmented matrix is
          <me>
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right].
          </me>
          This matrix is already in row-echelon form with leading-one pivots, so Gaussian elimination requires no row operations. The pivot variables are <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m> is free. The system is consistent. Back substitution gives <m>y=-z</m> from the second row, then <m>x=-y=z</m> from the first row.
        </p>
        <p>
          Thus every solution has the form
          <me>\mathbf{w}=\begin{bmatrix}z\\-z\\z\end{bmatrix}=z\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}, \qquad z\in\R,</me>
          and
          <me> U^\perp = \spans \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix} \right\} = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix}z\\-z\\z\end{bmatrix}: z \in \R \right\}. </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <fact xml:id="lemma-6-2-orthogonal">
      <title>Orthogonality after subtracting components</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1, \mathbf{q}_2, \ldots, \mathbf{q}_m\}</m> be an <term>orthonormal set</term> in <m>\R^n</m>. Given <m>\mathbf{x}</m> in <m>\R^n</m>, define
          <me>\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{x} - (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1 - (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2 - \cdots - (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m.</me>
          Then <m>\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k = 0</m> for <m>k = 1,2,\ldots,m</m>. Equivalently, <m>\mathbf{v}</m> lies in the orthogonal complement of the span:
          <me>\mathbf{v}\in\left(\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}\right)^\perp.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>c_i = \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{q}_i</m> for each <m>i</m>. Given <m>1 \leq k \leq m</m>, we calculate that
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k \amp = (\mathbf{x} - c_1\mathbf{q}_1 - c_2\mathbf{q}_2 - \cdots - c_m\mathbf{q}_m) \cdot \mathbf{q}_k</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k - c_1(\mathbf{q}_1 \cdot \mathbf{q}_k) - \cdots - c_k(\mathbf{q}_k \cdot \mathbf{q}_k) - \cdots - c_m(\mathbf{q}_m \cdot \mathbf{q}_k)</mrow>
          </md>
          Since the set is orthonormal, <m>\mathbf{q}_i \cdot \mathbf{q}_k = 0</m> for <m>i \neq k</m> and <m>\mathbf{q}_k \cdot \mathbf{q}_k=1</m>. Thus
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k \amp = \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k - c_k</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k - \mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{q}_k = 0</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <remark xml:id="remark-u4-expanding-within-span">
      <title>When the vector is already in the span</title>
      <p>
        If <m>\mathbf{x}\in\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}</m>, the expansion theorem (<xref ref="thm-u4-orthogonal-expansion"/>) gives
        <me>\mathbf{x}=(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1+\cdots+(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m.</me>
        Thus the vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in <xref ref="lemma-6-2-orthogonal"/> is <m>\mathbf{0}</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <definition xml:id="def-orthogonal-projection">
      <title>The orthogonal projection onto a subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>U</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>. Then the <term>orthogonal projection</term> of the vector <m>\mathbf{x} \in \R^n</m> onto <m>U</m> is the vector <m>\mathbf{y}=\proj_U(\mathbf{x}) \in U</m> which is <em>closest</em> to <m>\mathbf{x}</m>, in the sense that for any <m>\mathbf{z} \in U</m> with <m>\mathbf{z} \neq \mathbf{y}</m>, <m>\| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{y} \| \lt \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z} \|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      The following theorem gives us a practical way to calculate orthogonal projections.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-6-2projection">
      <title>Projection theorem</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>U</m> be a subspace of <m>\R^n</m> with <term>orthonormal basis</term> <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}</m>. For any vector <m>\mathbf{x} \in \R^n</m>, define
          <me>
            \mathbf{p} = (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1 + (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2 + \cdots + (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m.
          </me>
          Then <m>\mathbf{p}=\proj_U(\mathbf{x})</m>. The difference
          <me>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{p}=\mathbf{x}-\proj_U(\mathbf{x})</me>
          is called the <term>residual</term> and lies in <m>U^\perp</m>. In particular, if <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is already in <m>U</m>, then <m>\mathbf{x}</m> equals <m>\mathbf{p}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\{ \mathbf{q}_1, \dots, \mathbf{q}_m \}</m> is an <term>orthonormal basis</term> for <m>U</m>, and we define
          <me>\mathbf{p} = (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1 + (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2 + \cdots + (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m,</me>
          then <m>\mathbf{p}</m> is a linear combination of the basis vectors, so <m>\mathbf{p}\in U</m>. By <xref ref="lemma-6-2-orthogonal"/>, <m>\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{p}</m> is orthogonal to every basis vector, so <m>\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{p}\in U^\perp</m>. Thus if <m>\mathbf{z} \in U</m> and <m>\mathbf{z} \neq \mathbf{p}</m>, the Pythagorean theorem (<xref ref="thm-6-1-pythagoras-theorem"/>) tells us that
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z} \|^2 \amp = \| (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p}) + (\mathbf{p} - \mathbf{z}) \|^2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p} \|^2 + \| \mathbf{p} - \mathbf{z} \|^2
            </mrow>
          </md>.
          Since <m>\mathbf{p} \neq \mathbf{z}</m>, we find that
          <me>
            \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z} \|^2 \gt \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p} \|^2
          </me>,
          and taking square roots gives that
          <me>
            \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{z} \| \gt \| \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p} \|
          </me>.
          Thus the vector <m>\mathbf{p}</m> we have defined is equal to <m>\proj_U(\mathbf{x})</m>. Since we have already verified that <m>\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{p} = \mathbf{x} - \proj_U(\mathbf{x})</m> is in <m>U^\perp</m>, the theorem follows.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <p xml:id="video-thm-4-2-7-projection">
      Video walkthrough of <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>: <url href="https://math345-textbook.github.io/videos/thm-4-2-7-projection/">Watch: The projection theorem (1:15)</url>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Orthogonal projection decomposes the original vector into a vector in the subspace and a residual perpendicular to the subspace.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-u4-projection-residual">
      <caption>Projection and residual. Orthogonal projection writes <m>\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{p}+\mathbf{v}</m>, where <m>\mathbf{p}\in U</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}\in U^\perp</m>.</caption>
      <image source="figures/unit4/u4-projection-residual" width="90%">
        <shortdescription>A vector x is decomposed into its projection p along the subspace U and a perpendicular residual v. A right-angle marker shows that the residual is orthogonal to U.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <warning xml:id="warning-u4-projection-orthonormal">
      <title>Normalize before using the projection formula</title>
      <p>
        The formula in <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/> requires an <em>orthonormal</em> basis: mutual orthogonality alone is not enough. If you are given only an orthogonal set <m>\{\mathbf{f}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{f}_m\}</m> spanning <m>U</m>, first <xref ref="def-u1-unit-vector-normalization" text="custom">normalize</xref> each vector:
        <me>\mathbf{q}_i=\frac{\mathbf{f}_i}{\|\mathbf{f}_i\|},\qquad i=1,\ldots,m.</me>
        The vectors <m>\mathbf{q}_i</m> form an orthonormal basis of the same subspace. Use these unit vectors in the projection formula.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <note xml:id="note-u4-expansion-versus-projection">
      <title>Expansion versus projection</title>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="thm-u4-orthogonal-expansion" text="custom">expansion theorem</xref> and the <xref ref="thm-6-2projection" text="custom">projection theorem</xref> use the same orthonormal basis and the same coefficient formula. The difference in their hypotheses is that the expansion theorem requires <m>\mathbf{x}\in U=\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}</m>, so the sum equals <m>\mathbf{x}</m> itself. The projection theorem allows any <m>\mathbf{x}\in\R^n</m>; the sum equals <m>\proj_U(\mathbf{x})</m>, which need not equal <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. When <m>\mathbf{x}\in U</m>, the projection equals <m>\mathbf{x}</m> and the residual is zero, recovering the expansion theorem.
      </p>
    </note>

    <example xml:id="ex-u4-projection-span">
      <title>Projecting onto a span (U4-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\proj_U \mathbf{x}</m> where <m>U = \spans \{(1,1,0), (1,-1,0)\}</m> and <m>\mathbf{x} = (2,0,1)</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The vectors <m>\mathbf{f}_1 = (1,1,0)</m> and <m>\mathbf{f}_2 = (1,-1,0)</m> are already orthogonal, as
          <me>\mathbf{f}_1 \cdot \mathbf{f}_2 = 1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot (-1) + 0 \cdot 0 = 0</me>.
          Both vectors have length <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, so we first normalize them:
          <me>\mathbf{q}_1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{q}_2=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          They form an orthonormal basis for <m>U</m>. The projection coefficients are
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1 \amp =\frac{2\cdot1+0\cdot1+1\cdot0}{\sqrt{2}}=\sqrt{2},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2 \amp =\frac{2\cdot1+0\cdot(-1)+1\cdot0}{\sqrt{2}}=\sqrt{2}.</mrow>
          </md>
          Using <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>, we calculate that
          <md>
            <mrow>\proj_U \mathbf{x} \amp =(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1+(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp =\sqrt{2}\,\mathbf{q}_1+\sqrt{2}\,\mathbf{q}_2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp =\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-projection-plane-zero-coefficient">
      <title>Projection onto a plane (U4-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\proj_U\mathbf{x}</m> where <m>U=\spans\{(1,0,1),(1,0,-1)\}</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}=(1,2,1)</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The vectors <m>\mathbf{f}_1=(1,0,1)</m> and <m>\mathbf{f}_2=(1,0,-1)</m> are orthogonal because
          <me>\mathbf{f}_1\cdot\mathbf{f}_2=1\cdot1+0\cdot0+1\cdot(-1)=0.</me>
          Both have norm <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, so normalizing gives an orthonormal basis of <m>U</m>:
          <me>\mathbf{q}_1=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{q}_2=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\-1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          The projection coefficients are
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1 \amp =\frac{1\cdot1+2\cdot0+1\cdot1}{\sqrt{2}}=\sqrt{2},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2 \amp =\frac{1\cdot1+2\cdot0+1\cdot(-1)}{\sqrt{2}}=0.</mrow>
          </md>
          By <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{p}=\proj_U\mathbf{x} \amp =(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1+(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp =\sqrt{2}\,\mathbf{q}_1+0\,\mathbf{q}_2=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The projection onto <m>\spans\{\mathbf{q}_2\}</m> is the zero vector because <m>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2=0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-projection-line">
      <title>Projection onto a line (U4-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{f}=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix},\qquad
            \mathbf{x}=\begin{bmatrix}2\\5\end{bmatrix},\qquad
            L=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf{f}\}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\operatorname{proj}_L(\mathbf{x})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the residual <m>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{x}-\operatorname{proj}_L(\mathbf{x})</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Check that <m>\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{f}=0</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First, <m>\|\mathbf{f}\|=\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5</m>, so the unit vector
          <me>\mathbf{q}=\frac{\mathbf{f}}{5}=\begin{bmatrix}3/5\\4/5\end{bmatrix}</me>
          forms an orthonormal basis <m>\{\mathbf{q}\}</m> of <m>L</m>. We have <m>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}=2(3/5)+5(4/5)=26/5</m>. By <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>,
          <me>
            \operatorname{proj}_L(\mathbf{x})
            =
            (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q})\mathbf{q}
            =
            \frac{26}{25}\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}78/25\\104/25\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The residual is
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}2\\5\end{bmatrix}
            -
            \begin{bmatrix}78/25\\104/25\end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}-28/25\\21/25\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{f}
            =
            \left(-\frac{28}{25}\right)3+\left(\frac{21}{25}\right)4
            =
            0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-projection-line-code">
      <title>Projection onto a line in code (U4-LO3, U4-LO7)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following code first normalizes the direction vector, then computes the same projection and residual as the previous activity.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

f = np.array([3.0, 4.0])
x = np.array([2.0, 5.0])

q = f / np.linalg.norm(f)
p = (x @ q) * q
v = x - p

p, v, v @ f
]]></code></program>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which variable stores the projection?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which variable stores the residual?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why should <c>v @ f</c> be close to zero?</p></li>
          <li><p>What does <c>q = f / np.linalg.norm(f)</c> accomplish, and why is it needed before computing <c>(x @ q) * q</c>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The variable <c>p</c> stores the projection and <c>v</c> stores the residual. The value <c>v @ f</c> checks whether the residual is orthogonal to the direction of the line, so it should be close to zero. The assignment <c>q = f / np.linalg.norm(f)</c> makes <c>q</c> a unit vector spanning the same line as <c>f</c>. This normalization is needed because the dot-product formula in <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/> requires an orthonormal basis. The expression <c>(x @ q) * q</c> then computes
          <me>\proj_L(\mathbf{x})=(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q})\mathbf{q},\qquad L=\spans\{\mathbf{q}\}=\spans\{\mathbf{f}\}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Projection as an optimization problem</title>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="def-orthogonal-projection" text="custom">closest-point definition of projection</xref> naturally leads us to consider projection as an optimization problem.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u5-projection-as-optimization">
      <title>Projection as optimization (U4-LO3, U3-LO9)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            W=\operatorname{span}\left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}
            \right\}
            \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2,
            \qquad
            \mathbf{b}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          A general point of <m>W</m> has the form
          <me>
            \mathbf{w}(t)=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            t\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol marker="a">
          <li><p>Write the squared distance <m>g(t)=\|\mathbf{b}-\mathbf{w}(t)\|^2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>g'(t)</m> and find the critical point of <m>g</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Use <m>g''(t)</m> to classify the critical point. (Recall the second derivative test: if <m>g'(t_0)=0</m> and <m>g''</m> is continuous near <m>t_0</m>, then <m>g''(t_0)\gt 0</m> gives a local minimum, <m>g''(t_0)\lt 0</m> gives a local maximum, and <m>g''(t_0)=0</m> is inconclusive; see <url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/4-5-derivatives-and-the-shape-of-a-graph">OpenStax Calculus Volume 1, Section 4.5</url>.)</p></li>
          <li><p>What point of <m>W</m> is closest to <m>\mathbf{b}</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>How does this agree with <xref ref="fig-u4-projection-residual"/> and <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            \mathbf{b}-\mathbf{w}(t)=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2-t\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            g(t)=\|\mathbf{b}-\mathbf{w}(t)\|^2=(2-t)^2+9.
          </me>
          Differentiating gives
          <me>g'(t)=2(t-2).</me>
          The unique critical point is <m>t=2</m>. Since
          <me>g''(t)=2>0,</me>
          this critical point is a local minimum. Because <m>g</m> is an upward-opening quadratic, it is also the unique global minimum. Therefore the closest point in <m>W</m> is
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{b}}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          This agrees with <xref ref="fig-u4-projection-residual"/> and <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>: the residual is
          <me>
            \mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          For every <m>\mathbf{w}(t)\in W</m>,
          <me>
            (\mathbf{b}-\widehat{\mathbf{b}})\cdot\mathbf{w}(t)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            3
            \end{bmatrix}
            \cdot
            \begin{bmatrix}
            t\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix}
            =0\cdot t+3\cdot 0=0.
          </me>
          Therefore the residual is orthogonal to <m>W</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <example>
      <title>Deriving the projection formula through critical points</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{x}\in\R^n</m>, and let <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}</m> be an orthonormal basis of <m>U</m>. Every vector in <m>U</m> can be written as
        <me>
          \mathbf{u}(\mathbf{c})=\sum_{i=1}^m c_i\mathbf{q}_i,
          \qquad \mathbf{c}=(c_1,\ldots,c_m)\in\R^m.
        </me>
        Finding the closest vector to <m>\mathbf{x}</m> is therefore equivalent to choosing <m>\mathbf{c}</m> to minimize
        <me>g(\mathbf{c})=\|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{c})\|^2.</me>
        Squaring the distance does not change which vector is closest, and it gives a polynomial that is easy to differentiate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Expand the squared norm using the dot product. Since the basis vectors are orthonormal, the cross terms between distinct basis vectors vanish and each <m>\|\mathbf{q}_i\|^2=1</m>. Thus
        <md>
          <mrow>g(\mathbf{c}) \amp = \left(\mathbf{x}-\sum_{i=1}^m c_i\mathbf{q}_i\right)\cdot\left(\mathbf{x}-\sum_{i=1}^m c_i\mathbf{q}_i\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \|\mathbf{x}\|^2-2\sum_{i=1}^m c_i(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_i)+\sum_{i=1}^m c_i^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        The partial derivatives are
        <me>\frac{\partial g}{\partial c_i}=2c_i-2(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_i),\qquad i=1,\ldots,m.</me>
        Setting them all equal to zero gives the unique <xref ref="def-u3-critical-point" text="custom">critical point</xref> <m>\mathbf{c}^*</m>, whose entries are
        <me>c_i^*=\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_i,\qquad i=1,\ldots,m.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        To see that this critical point is the unique global minimum, <q>complete the squares</q>. Using <m>c_i^*=\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_i</m>, we obtain
        <md>
          <mrow>g(\mathbf{c}) \amp = \|\mathbf{x}\|^2+\sum_{i=1}^m\bigl(c_i^2-2c_ic_i^*\bigr)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \|\mathbf{x}\|^2+\sum_{i=1}^m\bigl(c_i^2-2c_ic_i^*+(c_i^*)^2-(c_i^*)^2\bigr)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \|\mathbf{x}\|^2-\sum_{i=1}^m(c_i^*)^2+\sum_{i=1}^m(c_i-c_i^*)^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = g(\mathbf{c}^*)+\sum_{i=1}^m(c_i-c_i^*)^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        In the last equality, we used <m>g(\mathbf{c}^*)=\|\mathbf{x}\|^2-\sum_{i=1}^m(c_i^*)^2</m>, obtained by substituting <m>\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{c}^*</m> into the expanded formula for <m>g</m>.
        The added terms are nonnegative and all vanish exactly when <m>\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{c}^*</m>. Consequently, the closest vector in <m>U</m> is
        <me>
          \mathbf{p}=\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{c}^*)
          =\sum_{i=1}^m(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_i)\mathbf{q}_i
          =\proj_U(\mathbf{x}).
        </me>
        This recovers the formula in <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/> by minimizing squared distance.
      </p>
    </example>

    <paragraphs xml:id="connection-u4-directional-derivatives-projection">
      <title>Directional derivatives as projection coefficients</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf v</m> be a unit vector. By <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>,
        <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\cdot\mathbf v\bigr)\mathbf v.</me>
        By <xref ref="thm-10-1-direction-in-terms-of-gradient"/>,
        <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=D_{\mathbf v}f(\mathbf a)\mathbf v.</me>
        Thus <m>D_{\mathbf v}f(\mathbf a)</m> is the signed projection coefficient of the gradient in the oriented direction <m>\mathbf v</m>. Its absolute value is the length of the projection.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u4-directional-derivative-projection-coefficient">
      <title>Directional derivative as a projection coefficient (U4-LO3, U3-LO8)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Recall the gradient and unit vectors from <xref ref="act-u5-dot-product-controls-direction"/>:
          <me>\nabla f(\mathbf a)=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix},</me>
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf v_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              \mathbf v_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf v_3=\frac15\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix},\qquad
              \mathbf v_4=-\frac15\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          For each unit vector <m>\mathbf v_i</m>, compute
          <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_i\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=D_{\mathbf v_i}f(\mathbf a)\mathbf v_i.</me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Which projection coefficients are positive, and which are negative?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why do <m>\mathbf v_3</m> and <m>\mathbf v_4</m> give the same projection vector but opposite directional derivatives?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From <xref ref="act-u5-dot-product-controls-direction"/>,
          <md>
            <mrow>D_{\mathbf v_1}f(\mathbf a)\amp=3,\qquad D_{\mathbf v_2}f(\mathbf a)=4,</mrow>
            <mrow>D_{\mathbf v_3}f(\mathbf a)\amp=5,\qquad D_{\mathbf v_4}f(\mathbf a)=-5.</mrow>
          </md>
          Therefore,
          <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_1\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=3\mathbf v_1=\begin{bmatrix}3\\0\end{bmatrix},</me>
          <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_2\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=4\mathbf v_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\4\end{bmatrix},</me>
          and
          <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_3\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=5\mathbf v_3=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          Since <m>\mathbf v_4=-\mathbf v_3</m>,
          <me>\operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf v_4\}}\bigl(\nabla f(\mathbf a)\bigr)=(-5)\mathbf v_4=\begin{bmatrix}3\\4\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          The vectors <m>\mathbf v_3</m> and <m>\mathbf v_4</m> span the same line, so projection onto that line gives the same vector. The directional derivatives have opposite signs because the two unit vectors give opposite orientations of the line.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs xml:id="attention-revisited">
      <title>Connection: score, then combine</title>
      <p>
        Projection and attention both use dot products to score directions and then use the resulting numbers in a linear combination.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let
        <me>
          Q=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{q}_1\amp\cdots\amp\mathbf{q}_k
          \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        have orthonormal columns. Then
        <me>
          Q^T\mathbf{x}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{x}\\
          \vdots\\
          \mathbf{q}_k^T\mathbf{x}
          \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        collects the directional coefficients of <m>\mathbf{x}</m>. Because each <m>\mathbf{q}_i</m> has length <m>1</m>, these dot products are the coordinates used in the projection. If <m>\mathbf{x}</m> also has length <m>1</m>, they are cosine similarities with the directions <m>\mathbf{q}_i</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Multiplying by <m>Q</m> combines the directions using those coefficients:
        <me>
          Q(Q^T\mathbf{x})
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^k
          (\mathbf{q}_i^T\mathbf{x})\mathbf{q}_i
          =
          \operatorname{proj}_{\operatorname{col}(Q)}(\mathbf{x}).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus projection follows the pattern
        <me>
          \text{score with }Q^T
          \quad\longrightarrow\quad
          \text{combine with }Q.
        </me>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-u4-score-then-combine">
        <caption>Score, then combine. Projection uses <m>Q^T\mathbf{x}</m> to score orthonormal directions and <m>Q(Q^T\mathbf{x})</m> to combine them. Attention uses query-key scores, converts them into weights, and combines value vectors.</caption>
        <image source="figures/unit4/u4-score-then-combine" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>Two parallel pipelines. Projection sends x to Q transpose x and then to Q times Q transpose x. Attention sends a query q to Kq, then to weights alpha, and then to alpha transpose V.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p xml:id="ai-u4-attention-not-projection">
        Attention has the same broad pattern. Query-key dot products produce scores, a weighting rule converts the scores into weights, and the weights combine value vectors. Projection scores and combines the same orthonormal directions. Attention scores key vectors and then combines value vectors, which may be different vectors. Projection also chooses its coefficients so that the residual is orthogonal to the subspace. Attention is therefore not generally an orthogonal projection, but both computations are built from dot products and linear combinations.
      </p>

      <activity xml:id="act-u4-attention-or-projection">
        <title>Dot-product scores as projection coordinates (U4-LO1, U4-LO3)</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              \mathbf{q}_1
              =
              \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              1\\
              0
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{q}_2
              =
              \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              -1\\
              0
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{x}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\\
              0\\
              1
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and let
            <me>
              Q=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              \mathbf{q}_1\amp\mathbf{q}_2
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Verify that the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the score vector
                <me>\mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
                <me>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}=Q\mathbf{c}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the residual
                <me>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{x}-\widehat{\mathbf{x}},</me>
                and verify that <m>Q^T\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Explain why the entries of <m>\mathbf{c}</m> can be read both as dot-product scores and as coordinates of the projection.</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain how the calculation follows the pattern <q>score, then combine.</q> What extra condition identifies <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> as an orthogonal projection?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The two columns have length <m>1</m>, and
            <me>
              \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{q}_2
              =
              \frac{1}{2}(1-1)=0.
            </me>
            Thus the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal.
          </p>
          <p>
            The score vector is
            <me>
              \mathbf{c}
              =
              Q^T\mathbf{x}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{x}\\
              \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{x}
              \end{bmatrix}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              \sqrt{2}\\
              \sqrt{2}
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Combining the columns of <m>Q</m> using these scores gives
            <me>
              \widehat{\mathbf{x}}
              =
              Q\mathbf{c}
              =
              \sqrt{2}\mathbf{q}_1+\sqrt{2}\mathbf{q}_2
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\\
              0\\
              0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            The residual is
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}
              =
              \mathbf{x}-\widehat{\mathbf{x}}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0\\
              1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Therefore
            <me>
              Q^T\mathbf{v}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              0\\
              0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Each entry of <m>\mathbf{c}</m> is a dot product with one of the unit directions, so it is both a directional score and a coordinate used to reconstruct the projection. The calculation first scores <m>\mathbf{x}</m> with <m>Q^T</m>, then combines the columns of <m>Q</m> using those scores. The condition <m>Q^T\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</m> says that the residual is orthogonal to every column of <m>Q</m>. This is the extra condition that identifies <m>\widehat{\mathbf{x}}</m> as the orthogonal projection.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Gram-Schmidt orthonormalization</title>
    <p>
      The Gram-Schmidt algorithm converts a basis into an orthonormal basis of the same subspace through a sequence of projection, subtraction, measurement, and normalization steps.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u4-extending-orthonormal-set">
      <title>Expanding an orthonormal set</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m\}</m> be an <term>orthonormal set</term> in <m>\R^n</m>, and let <m>\mathbf{x}\in\R^n</m> lie <term>outside its span</term>. Define
          <me>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{x}-(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1-\cdots-(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m.</me>
          Then <m>\mathbf{v}\neq\mathbf{0}</m>. <term>Normalize</term> it by setting
          <me>\mathbf{q}_{m+1}=\frac{\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{v}\|}.</me>
          The enlarged set <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m,\mathbf{q}_{m+1}\}</m> is an <term>orthonormal set</term>, and the span is preserved:
          <me>\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m,\mathbf{q}_{m+1}\}=\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m,\mathbf{x}\}.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</m>, then <m>\mathbf{x}=(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1+\cdots+(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m</m> lies in the original span, contrary to the hypothesis. Thus <m>\mathbf{v}\neq\mathbf{0}</m> and we can normalize it. By <xref ref="lemma-6-2-orthogonal"/>, <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is orthogonal to every <m>\mathbf{q}_k</m>. The vector <m>\mathbf{q}_{m+1}</m> has norm one and remains orthogonal to every <m>\mathbf{q}_k</m>, so the enlarged set is orthonormal.
        </p>
        <p>
          The formula for <m>\mathbf{q}_{m+1}</m> expresses it as a linear combination of <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_m,\mathbf{x}</m>. Conversely,
          <me>\mathbf{x}=(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1+\cdots+(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_m)\mathbf{q}_m+\|\mathbf{v}\|\mathbf{q}_{m+1}.</me>
          Each set therefore spans the other, proving the equality of spans.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <activity xml:id="subsec-orthogonal-projections-7">
      <title>Extending an orthonormal set to a basis (U4-LO2)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          First normalize the orthogonal vectors
          <me>\mathbf{f}_1 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix} \quad\text{and}\quad \mathbf{f}_2 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}</me>
          to obtain an orthonormal set <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2\}</m> in <m>\R^3</m>. Use <xref ref="fact-u4-extending-orthonormal-set"/> with <m>\mathbf{x}=(1,1,2)</m> to extend this set to an orthonormal basis <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2,\mathbf{q}_3\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Both given vectors have norm <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, so we first set
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_1 \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_2 \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          Their dot product is zero, so they form an orthonormal set. We calculate
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1 \amp = \frac{1+1}{\sqrt{2}}=\sqrt{2},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2 \amp = \frac{1-1}{\sqrt{2}}=0.</mrow>
          </md>
          Using <xref ref="lemma-6-2-orthogonal"/>, we obtain
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v} \amp = \mathbf{x}-(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1-(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix}-\sqrt{2}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}\right)-0\mathbf{q}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\2\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          Since <m>\|\mathbf{v}\|=2</m>, normalize once more:
          <me>\mathbf{q}_3=\frac{\mathbf{v}}{\|\mathbf{v}\|}=\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.</me>
          By <xref ref="lemma-6-2-orthogonal"/>, <m>\mathbf{q}_3</m> is orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{q}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{q}_2</m>. Since <m>\|\mathbf{q}_3\|=1</m>, the set <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2,\mathbf{q}_3\}</m> is orthonormal. These three vectors are linearly independent by <xref ref="thm-6-1-orthogonality-independent"/>, so they form an orthonormal basis of <m>\R^3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      We use an original column <m>\mathbf{a}_j</m>, its projection <m>\mathbf{p}_j</m>, the orthogonal residual <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m>, the new unit direction <m>\mathbf{q}_j</m>, and coefficients <m>r_{ij}</m>. Repeatedly applying <xref ref="fact-u4-extending-orthonormal-set"/> to the normalized vectors <m>\mathbf{q}_i</m> gives the algorithm below.
    </p>

    <algorithm xml:id="alg-u4-gram-schmidt">
      <title>Gram-Schmidt algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <strong>Input:</strong> A matrix
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{a}_1 \amp \mathbf{a}_2 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{a}_n
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          with linearly independent columns.
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>j=1,\ldots,n</m>:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><strong>Current column.</strong> Start with <m>\mathbf{a}_j</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>Project.</strong> For each <m>i \lt j</m>, compute
              <me>r_{ij}=\mathbf{q}_i^T\mathbf{a}_j.</me>
              Then assemble
              <me>\mathbf{p}_j=\sum_{i \lt j}r_{ij}\mathbf{q}_i.</me>
              For the first column, <m>\mathbf{p}_1=\mathbf{0}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>Subtract.</strong>
              <me>\mathbf{v}_j=\mathbf{a}_j-\mathbf{p}_j.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>Measure.</strong>
              <me>r_{jj}=\|\mathbf{v}_j\|.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong>Normalize.</strong>
              <me>\mathbf{q}_j=\frac{\mathbf{v}_j}{r_{jj}}.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>Output:</strong> The vectors <m>\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_n</m> are orthonormal, and
          <me>
            \spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_j\}
            =
            \spans\{\mathbf{a}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{a}_j\}
          </me>
          at every stage.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </algorithm>

    <p xml:id="practice-u4-perfectly-normal">
      <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/PerfectlyNormal/" visual="Perfectly Normal">Practice Gram-Schmidt geometrically in Perfectly Normal.</url>
    </p>

    <warning xml:id="warn-u4-gram-schmidt-zero-residual">
      <p>
        If <m>r_{jj}=0</m>, then <m>\mathbf{a}_j</m> is already in the span of the earlier columns. The input columns are not linearly independent, and no new unit direction is produced.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <p>
      The following theorem summarizes the properties of the vectors produced by <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt"/>.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-6-2-gram-schmidt">
      <title>Properties of the Gram-Schmidt output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt"/> to a matrix <m>A=[\mathbf{a}_1\ \cdots\ \mathbf{a}_n]</m> with linearly independent columns, and let <m>U=\spans\{\mathbf{a}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{a}_n\}</m>. For each <m>k=1,\ldots,n</m>, write <m>U_k=\spans\{\mathbf{a}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{a}_k\}</m>. The output through step <m>k</m> satisfies:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The span is preserved:
              <me>\spans\{\mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_k\}=\spans\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_k\}=U_k.</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>The normalized vectors <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_k\}</m> form an orthonormal basis of <m>U_k</m>.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p>In particular, <m>\{\mathbf{q}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{q}_n\}</m> is an orthonormal basis of <m>U</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="act-u4-gram-schmidt-row-space">
      <title>Gram–Schmidt for a column space (U4-LO2)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply the Gram-Schmidt algorithm to the columns of <m>A</m>, where
          <me>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</me>.
          At each stage, record the projection coefficients, projection, residual, residual length, and normalized direction. Give an orthogonal basis and an orthonormal basis for <m>\col(A)</m> (recall <xref ref="def-5-3-column-and-row-space"/>).
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Compute <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>, <m>r_{11}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{q}_1</m>.</li>
          <li>Compute <m>r_{12}</m>, <m>\mathbf{p}_2</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>, <m>r_{22}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{q}_2</m>.</li>
          <li>Compute <m>r_{13}</m>, <m>r_{23}</m>, <m>\mathbf{p}_3</m>, <m>\mathbf{v}_3</m>, <m>r_{33}</m>, and <m>\mathbf{q}_3</m>.</li>
          <li>Give an orthogonal basis for <m>\col(A)</m> from the residuals.</li>
          <li>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\col(A)</m> from the unit vectors.</li>
          <li>Explain why both bases span <m>\col(A)</m>.</li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let the columns of <m>A</m> be
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{a}_1 \amp = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{a}_2 \amp = \begin{bmatrix}3\\2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{a}_3 \amp = \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          For the first column,
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v}_1 \amp = \mathbf{a}_1 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>r_{11} \amp = \|\mathbf{v}_1\|=2,</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_1 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_1}{r_{11}}=\frac12\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          For the second column,
          <md>
            <mrow>r_{12} \amp = \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_2=2,</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{p}_2 \amp = r_{12}\mathbf{q}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v}_2 \amp = \mathbf{a}_2-\mathbf{p}_2=\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>r_{22} \amp = \|\mathbf{v}_2\|=\sqrt{10},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_2 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{r_{22}}=\frac1{\sqrt{10}}\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          For the third column,
          <md>
            <mrow>r_{13} \amp = \mathbf{q}_1^T\mathbf{a}_3=0,</mrow>
            <mrow>r_{23} \amp = \mathbf{q}_2^T\mathbf{a}_3=\frac3{\sqrt{10}},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{p}_3 \amp = r_{13}\mathbf{q}_1+r_{23}\mathbf{q}_2=\frac3{10}\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v}_3 \amp = \mathbf{a}_3-\mathbf{p}_3=\frac1{10}\begin{bmatrix}4\\-3\\7\\-6\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>r_{33} \amp = \|\mathbf{v}_3\|=\frac{\sqrt{110}}{10},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_3 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_3}{r_{33}}=\frac1{\sqrt{110}}\begin{bmatrix}4\\-3\\7\\-6\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          An orthogonal basis from the residuals is
          <me>
            \{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3\}=
            \left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix},
            \frac1{10}\begin{bmatrix}4\\-3\\7\\-6\end{bmatrix}
            \right\}.
          </me>
          The corresponding orthonormal basis is
          <me>
            \{\mathbf{q}_1,\mathbf{q}_2,\mathbf{q}_3\}=
            \left\{
            \frac12\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix},
            \frac1{\sqrt{10}}\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix},
            \frac1{\sqrt{110}}\begin{bmatrix}4\\-3\\7\\-6\end{bmatrix}
            \right\}.
          </me>
          The columns <m>\mathbf{a}_1,\mathbf{a}_2,\mathbf{a}_3</m> span <m>\col(A)</m>. By <xref ref="thm-6-2-gram-schmidt"/>, Gram-Schmidt preserves each successive span. Therefore the residuals form an orthogonal basis of <m>\col(A)</m>, and their normalized unit vectors form an orthonormal basis of <m>\col(A)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <activity>
      <title>The closest point on a plane (U4-LO3)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the point in the plane defined by the equation <m>x - y - 2z = 0</m>, which is closest to the point <m>(1,2,-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>U</m> be the plane. Solving its equation for <m>x</m> gives <m>x=y+2z</m>. If we set <m>y=s</m> and <m>z=t</m>, then every vector in <m>U</m> has the form
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \begin{bmatrix}s+2t\\s\\t\end{bmatrix}
              \amp = s\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
              + t\begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Therefore
          <me>
            U=\spans\left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
            \right\}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
            \quad\text{and}\quad
            \mathbf{u}_2=\begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Applying the Gram-Schmidt process from <xref ref="alg-u4-gram-schmidt"/> gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \mathbf{v}_1 \amp = \mathbf{u}_1
              = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \mathbf{v}_2 \amp = \mathbf{u}_2
              -\frac{\mathbf{u}_2\cdot\mathbf{v}_1}{\|\mathbf{v}_1\|^2}\mathbf{v}_1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix}2\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
              -\frac{2}{2}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Thus <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2\}</m> is an orthogonal basis of <m>U</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          First normalize this basis:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_1 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_1}{\|\mathbf{v}_1\|}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix},</mrow>
            <mrow>\mathbf{q}_2 \amp = \frac{\mathbf{v}_2}{\|\mathbf{v}_2\|}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
          For the given point <m>\mathbf{x}=(1,2,-1)</m>,
          <me>\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1=\frac{3}{\sqrt{2}},\qquad \mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2=-\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}.</me>
          Applying <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/> to this orthonormal basis gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \proj_U(\mathbf{x}) \amp =
              (\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_1)\mathbf{q}_1
              +(\mathbf{x}\cdot\mathbf{q}_2)\mathbf{q}_2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{3}{\sqrt{2}}\mathbf{q}_1-\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\mathbf{q}_2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{3}{2}\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}
              -\frac{2}{3}\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix}5/6\\13/6\\-2/3\end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Hence the point in the plane closest to <m>(1,2,-1)</m> is <m>(5/6,13/6,-2/3)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>
